  "strategy": "Approach and Rationale\n\n**Central Idea**:  \nOur approach leverages the heat equation to extract the essential features of the optimal function. The heat equation smooths the function while preserving areas of interest. We initialize from a known good starting point, then use gradient-based optimization to refine the solution.\n\n**Key Features**:\n- Start from the provided parent solution (if available)\n- Precompute gradient information using the heat equation for faster convergence\n- Use adaptive line searches to tune the step size\n- Implement coordinate descent for breaking symmetry-induced local minima\n- Employ temperature scaling to gradually improve the solution\n\n**Rationale**:  \n1. The optimal function structure can be described as having sharp peaks (spikes) interspersed with regions of low value\n2. The current best solution (0.380935) needs refinement through structured optimization\n3. Heat-based gradient estimation provides a balance between speed and accuracy\n4. Line search optimization can avoid local minima more effectively than fixed step sizes\n5. Temperature scaling improves convergence properties\n\n**Limitations**:\n- Gradient computation is expensive due to correlation calculations\n- May get stuck in local minima if initial temperatures are too high\n- Optimized hyperparameters are domain-specific and may require tuning\n\nMathematical Specification\n\nVariables and Notation:\n- h: Piecewise constant function on [0,2] representing the step function\n- n: Number of sample points (resolution)\n- dx = 2/n: Spacing between grid points\n- \u222bh(x)dx = 1: Integral constraint\n- C_5 = max_k \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx: Objective to minimize\n\nVariables:\n- Let h be represented as N points on [0,2]\n- Decision variables: h\u2081, h\u2082, ..., h_N \u2208 [0,1] with \u2211h\u1d62\u2022dx = 1\n\nConstraints:\n1. 0 \u2264 h\u2081, h\u2082, ..., h_N \u2264 1\n2. (1/(2N))\u2211\u1d62h\u1d62 = 1/N * (n/2) = 1/2 for integral constraint\n\nExact Objective Translation:\nThe Python evaluator uses:\n  np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full') gives a vector of 2N-1 values\n  C_5 = max(absolute correlation values) * dx\n\nImplementation Procedure\n\n1. Initialization:\n   - If parent solution available, use it as the initial guess\n   - Else, generate a random feasible function h\u2080 with \u2225h\u2080\u2225=1\n   - n = 1024 (default high resolution)\n\n2. Gradient Computation:\n   - Use heat_kernel(x) = exp(-\u03b5|x|) to compute gradient estimates\n   - Compute approximate gradient via finite differences using the heat kernel\n   - con_fun(h) = \u2211h\u1d62 - n/2\n   - con_jac(h) = 1.0 for all components\n\n3. Optimization:\n   - Use global temperature T0 = 0.5\n   - Activate coordinate descent (MC=100 steps) at each temperature level\n   - Perform uphill moves with probability proportional to exp(-\u0394/T)\n   - Apply simulated annealing with T decreasing as 1/ln(i)\n   - Compute gradients using central differences\n\n4. Local Refinement:\n   - After downhill moves, use line search along gradient direction\n   - Line search: max\u209b\u2208[0,1] f(h+sp)\n   - Increase n by doubling in each refinement phase\n\nSearch Choices and Variations\n\nDefault Configuration:\n- Temperature schedule: T = T0 / ln(t + 1)\n- Line search interval: [0.5, 5.0] multiples of initial step size\n- Coordinate descent: max_rounds=100, improvement_threshold=1e-8\n- Basis components: 128 components for low-rank function representation\n\nImportant hyperparameters with ranges:\n- Temperature adjustment: \u03b1 \u2208 [0.9, 0.99]\n- Line search scale: \u03b2 \u2208 [0.1, 0.5]\n- Coordinate descent steps: MC \u2208 [20, 200]\n\nCompute Budget and Efficiency\n\nEstimates for n=1024 (2048, \u221e):\n- Heat-gradient computation: O(n\u00b2) \u2248 1e9 operations\n- Local optimization: 500 function evaluations\n- Coordinate descent: ~2000 gradient probes\n\nKey timing distribution:\n- Function evaluation: 80%\n- Gradient computation: 15%\n- Optimization search: 5%\n\nConstraint Enforcement:\n   - Repair integral using Lagrange multiplier method\n   - Clip values to [0,1] post-step\n   - Use quadratic energy function for smooth transitions\n\nTermination conditions:\n   - \u0394C\u2085 < 1e-7\n   - Time budget exceeded\n   - No improvement for 500 function evaluations\n\nValidation and Return Contract\n\nFinal Validation:\n   - Check integral: \u2211h\u1d62 = n/2 exactly\n   - Ensure 0 \u2264 h\u1d62 \u2264 1 for all i\n   - Recompute C\u2085 using full correlation\n   - Binary and limit checks\n\nFallback Strategy:\n   - If line search fails completely, fall back to coordinate only\n   - If gradient computation crashes, use finite-differences with small \u03b5\n   - Return parent solution on critical failure\n\nDisplay:\n   - Print progress every 100 gradient steps\n   - Output final C\u2085 value\n   - Show iteration improvement plot",
